// tests/extension.bx — every version an editor artefact declares, checked against the format it paints.
//
//     burxt build tests/extension.bx -o extension
//     ./extension
//     ./extension --prove-it     # the negative control
//
// BMX_PACK names the packer to drive; without it, this builds `editors/vscode/pack.bx`.
//
// **Thirty commits changed the package and the version never moved off 0.1.0.** Measured, not suspected:
// `git log -- editors/vscode/bmx-0.1.0.vsix` counted thirty, and unzipping the first one against the last
// gives two grammars — an 8,931-byte one that knows only `::: card`, and a 13,235-byte one that knows
// `:name:` / `:!name:` — both declaring version 0.1.0.
//
// **That is the worst available failure, because the mechanism that should fix it is the mechanism that
// hides it.** VS Code decides whether to offer an update by comparing versions. Anyone who installed the
// extension before the 0.7 respelling has a highlighter that paints today's documents wrong, and their
// editor tells them they are current. Reinstalling changes nothing. There is no symptom except colour.
//
// **And the cause was ergonomic, which is why no amount of care would have fixed it.** The artefact was
// called `bmx-<version>.vsix`, so the version appeared in seven places: two READMEs, three doc pages, the
// packer's own header, and CI. Bumping it broke every install command in the repository. **A version that
// is expensive to change is a version that does not change** — so the filename lost its version
// (`bmx.vsix`, stable forever) and the version now lives only where a tool reads it.
//
// That fix removes the friction. This file removes the option:
//
//   * **The extension's `major.minor` must equal `SPEC.md`'s.** `SPEC.md`'s title is already the one place
//     the version is decided — `tests/version.bx` checks every prose claim against it. This is the same
//     rule reaching the one claim that check cannot see, because it is JSON inside a zip.
//   * **The patch is free**, so a packaging fix does not need a format release. What is not free is a
//     format release that leaves the extension behind, which is the thing that happened.
//   * **The committed artefact must be the one the packer writes**, byte for byte — which required making
//     the pack reproducible first, because it was not. Three entries carried the current time, so the
//     bytes moved on every run and a stale commit was undetectable. Nothing detected it: CI repacks and
//     then inspects the result, overwriting the evidence one line before looking for it. **A check that
//     regenerates the artefact it is verifying cannot see a stale one.**
//   * **And the reproducibility is asserted as a property, not by packing twice.** The first version of
//     that check packed twice in a row and PASSED on the non-reproducible packer, because a zip stores
//     timestamps at two-second granularity and back-to-back runs share a bucket. A three-second sleep
//     exposed it. **A test that can only fail when it happens to straddle a boundary is a test that
//     reports success.** One fixed stamp per entry cannot pass by accident.
//   * **Every filename the documentation tells someone to install must exist.** This is the check that
//     matters for the fix itself: the whole point of a stable name is that six documents keep pointing at
//     a real file, and the way to lose that is to rename it again.
//   * **The language server too**, found by grepping for the version I had just removed: it answered
//     `initialize` with `0.1.0` while producing 0.12 diagnostics, and its own test asserted the *name* —
//     the half that never changes. Read from source rather than over the wire, which is the weaker of the
//     two and enough for the failure that actually happens: nobody bumps it.
//   * **And the install PATH, which is the same promise one step out.** A page naming a `.vsix` that is
//     not in the tree is checked above; a page naming an extensions *directory* that does not exist on the
//     reader's machine is the same defect and was not. `~/.vscode/extensions` is wrong in every remote
//     window — VS Code Server loads extensions from the remote host — and **the machine BMX is developed
//     on has no such directory.** The star-burxt session lost Andre a day to exactly this sentence in
//     star's docs: three finished grammars, packed correctly, never installed, no error to search for
//     because a `cp` into a path whose parent exists succeeds silently. `code --install-extension` is the
//     instruction that removes the class, because it resolves the directory and prints which machine it
//     used.
//
//     So: every page that tells somebody to install must name `code --install-extension`, and a page
//     naming a raw extensions directory must name the remote one beside it. **This is a lint on prose,
//     which is the only kind of check available** — CI has no VS Code, so the instruction cannot be
//     executed, only kept honest.
//
// ---- the port ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ported from `tests/extension.py`. Verified verdict- and byte-identical against it while both the Python
// packer and the Python checker still existed, then flipped with the packer in one commit.
//
// **It reads the archive with `lib/zip.bx`, which exists because of this pair of files** — and its header
// says the reason out loud: a checker that reads one copy of duplicated ZIP metadata is not an oracle,
// because a ZIP stores every entry's metadata twice and `zipfile` resolves through the central directory
// and never looks at the local header. Zeroing a local CRC left an independent test PASSING. Here the
// content check goes through `zip_content`, which walks the LOCAL header and verifies the CRC — so the two
// copies are both read, by two different assertions, which is what the Python could not do at all.
use "std/files.bx";
use "std/json.bx";
use "std/os.bx";
use "std/string.bx";
use "std/zip.bx";

class Claim { ok: Bool, ok_line: String, fail_line: String }

const PKG: String = "editors/vscode/package.json";
const VSIX: String = "editors/vscode/bmx.vsix";
const PACK: String = "editors/vscode/pack.bx";
const LOCAL: String = "~/.vscode/extensions";
const REMOTE: String = "~/.vscode-server/extensions";

// Where somebody is told to install it. A promise to install a file is a promise the file is there.
function pages() -> [String] {
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let a: Int = push(out, "docs/editor.md");
    let b: Int = push(out, "docs/install.md");
    let c: Int = push(out, "editors/README.md");
    let d: Int = push(out, "editors/vscode/README.md");
    let e: Int = push(out, PACK);
    let f: Int = push(out, "tools/check.sh");
    let g: Int = push(out, ".github/workflows/ci.yml");
    return out;
}

pure function is_digit(b: Int) -> Bool {
    return b >= 48 && b <= 57;
}

pure function version_end(text: String, from: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    while i < len(text) && is_digit(byte_at(text, i)) {
        i += 1;
    }
    if i == from || i >= len(text) || byte_at(text, i) != 46 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    i += 1;
    let after: Int = i;
    while i < len(text) && is_digit(byte_at(text, i)) {
        i += 1;
    }
    if i == after {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    return i;
}

function must_read(path: String) -> String touches files {
    let mutable text: String = "";
    match file_read_maybe(path) {
        None => {
            print_error("cannot read " + path + " — run this from the repository root");
            exit(2);
        }
        Some(said) => { text = said; }
    }
    return text;
}

// The one place the format's version is decided: the normative document's title.
function spec_version() -> String touches files {
    let text: String = must_read("SPEC.md");
    let lines: [String] = string_split(text, "\n");
    let mutable first: String = "";
    if len(lines) > 0 {
        first = lines[0];
    }
    let at: Int = string_find(first, "BMX ");
    if at >= 0 {
        let ends: Int = version_end(first, at + 4);
        if ends > 0 {
            return substring(first, at + 4, ends - at - 4);
        }
    }
    print_error("SPEC.md's first line does not name a version, so nothing can be checked against it");
    exit(1);
    return "";
}

function field_of(path: String, name: String) -> String touches files {
    let text: String = must_read(path);
    match json_parse(text) {
        Error(why) => {
            print_error(path + " is not JSON: " + why);
            exit(2);
        }
        Ok(parsed) => {
            match json_at(parsed, name) {
                None => { return ""; }
                Some(value) => {
                    match json_as_text(value) {
                        None => { return ""; }
                        Some(said) => { return said; }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return "";
}

function json_text_field(text: String, name: String) -> String {
    match json_parse(text) {
        Error(why) => { return ""; }
        Ok(parsed) => {
            match json_at(parsed, name) {
                None => { return ""; }
                Some(value) => {
                    match json_as_text(value) {
                        None => { return ""; }
                        Some(said) => { return said; }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// `<Identity[^>]*Version="([^"]+)"`
pure function identity_version(manifest: String) -> String {
    let at: Int = string_find(manifest, "<Identity");
    if at < 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let mutable close: Int = string_find_from(manifest, ">", at);
    if close < 0 {
        close = len(manifest);
    }
    let tag: String = substring(manifest, at, close - at);
    let mark: Int = string_find(tag, "Version=\"");
    if mark < 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let start: Int = mark + 9;
    let ends: Int = string_find_from(tag, "\"", start);
    if ends < 0 {
        return "";
    }
    return substring(tag, start, ends - start);
}

// The DOS date and time an entry carries, as Python's `date_time` tuple reads.
pure function stamp_text(entry: ZipRead) -> String {
    let year: Int = 1980 + divide_floor(entry.dos_date, 512);
    let month: Int = remainder(divide_floor(entry.dos_date, 32), 16);
    let day: Int = remainder(entry.dos_date, 32);
    let hour: Int = divide_floor(entry.dos_time, 2048);
    let minute: Int = remainder(divide_floor(entry.dos_time, 32), 64);
    let second: Int = remainder(entry.dos_time, 32) * 2;
    return "(" + to_string(year) + ", " + to_string(month) + ", " + to_string(day) + ", "
            + to_string(hour) + ", " + to_string(minute) + ", " + to_string(second) + ")";
}

pure function has(items: [String], needle: String) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(items) {
        if items[i] == needle {
            return true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return false;
}

function sorted_unique(items: [String]) -> [String] {
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(items) {
        if !has(out, items[i]) {
            let a: Int = push(out, items[i]);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    let mutable m: Int = 0;
    while m < len(out) {
        let mutable best: Int = m;
        let mutable n: Int = m + 1;
        while n < len(out) {
            if string_compare(out[n], out[best]) < 0 {
                best = n;
            }
            n += 1;
        }
        let hold: String = out[m];
        out[m] = out[best];
        out[best] = hold;
        m += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// `re.split(r"[\s`'\"(),]+", line)` then `.strip("`'\".,;:!?()")`.
//
// **A SPLIT, not a character class, and that distinction is the day's best transferable finding.** This
// was `\b([\w.-]+\.vsix)\b`, and `code --install-extension bmx-<version>.vsix` matched NOTHING: the
// character before `.vsix` is `>`, which the class does not admit, so the scan could not reach the name
// and a wrong filename on a real install line passed silently. Verified before changing it.
//
// **A character class enumerates what a filename may contain; splitting on delimiters accepts whatever is
// actually there.** Prose contains things filenames do not — placeholders, ellipses, a name inside
// parentheses — and each of those is a hole in a class and none is a hole in a split.
//
// **And the lesson's second half, which is symmetric and cost two measurements to find:**
//
//     A class misses what it did not enumerate. A split accepts what it should not.
//
// The class missed `bmx-<version>.vsix` because `>` was not in it. The split then failed at the other end
// of the same token: pointed at `editors/README.md` — the page about how a HOST extends BMX — a legitimate
// `code --install-extension star-burxt.vsix` was reported as a missing file of ours. So the split is
// filtered to OUR artefact, by the name `package.json` declares rather than a literal.
pure function is_delimiter(b: Int) -> Bool {
    return b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 10 || b == 13 || b == 96 || b == 39 || b == 34 || b == 40
            || b == 41 || b == 44;
}

pure function is_trimmed(b: Int) -> Bool {
    return b == 96 || b == 39 || b == 34 || b == 46 || b == 44 || b == 59 || b == 58 || b == 33
            || b == 63 || b == 40 || b == 41;
}

pure function trimmed(token: String) -> String {
    let mutable from: Int = 0;
    let mutable to: Int = len(token);
    while from < to && is_trimmed(byte_at(token, from)) {
        from += 1;
    }
    while to > from && is_trimmed(byte_at(token, to - 1)) {
        to -= 1;
    }
    return substring(token, from, to - from);
}

pure function basename(path: String) -> String {
    let slash: Int = file_last_slash(path);
    if slash < 0 {
        return path;
    }
    return substring(path, slash + 1, len(path) - slash - 1);
}

// Every `.vsix` of ours that an install line promises.
pure function promised(text: String, ours: String) -> [String] {
    let mutable out: [String] = [];
    let lines: [String] = string_split(text, "\n");
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(lines) {
        let line: String = lines[i];
        if string_contains(line, "install-extension") {
            let mutable at: Int = 0;
            while at < len(line) {
                while at < len(line) && is_delimiter(byte_at(line, at)) {
                    at += 1;
                }
                let start: Int = at;
                while at < len(line) && !is_delimiter(byte_at(line, at)) {
                    at += 1;
                }
                if at > start {
                    let token: String = trimmed(substring(line, start, at - start));
                    if string_ends_with(token, ".vsix") && string_contains(token, ours) {
                        let a: Int = push(out, basename(token));
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// `writes\s+(\S+\.vsix)\s+here` over the packer's own text.
pure function usage_promise(text: String) -> String {
    let mutable from: Int = 0;
    while true {
        let at: Int = string_find_from(text, "writes", from);
        if at < 0 {
            return "(no `writes … here` line)";
        }
        from = at + 1;
        let mutable i: Int = at + 6;
        let gap: Int = i;
        while i < len(text) && string_is_space(byte_at(text, i)) {
            i += 1;
        }
        if i == gap {
            continue;
        }
        let start: Int = i;
        while i < len(text) && !string_is_space(byte_at(text, i)) {
            i += 1;
        }
        let token: String = substring(text, start, i - start);
        if !string_ends_with(token, ".vsix") {
            continue;
        }
        let after: Int = i;
        while i < len(text) && string_is_space(byte_at(text, i)) {
            i += 1;
        }
        if i == after {
            continue;
        }
        if string_matches_at(text, "here", i) {
            return token;
        }
    }
    return "(no `writes … here` line)";
}

pure function plural(n: Int, word: String) -> String {
    if n == 1 {
        return word;
    }
    return word + "s";
}

// ---- the program ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let mutable prove: Bool = false;
let mutable a: Int = 1;
while a < os_arg_count() {
    if os_arg(a) == "--prove-it" {
        prove = true;
    }
    a += 1;
}

let mutable claims: [Claim] = [];
let want: String = spec_version();
let source_version: String = field_of(PKG, "version");
let ours: String = field_of(PKG, "name");
let mutable declared: String = source_version;
if prove {
    // The control is the defect exactly as it happened: a format release ships, the extension is not
    // bumped, and the packaged grammar is newer than the version that describes it.
    declared = "0.1.0";
}

let k1: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: string_starts_with(declared, want + "."),
        ok_line: "the extension declares " + declared + ", which is SPEC.md's " + want,
        fail_line: "the extension declares " + declared + " and SPEC.md says the format is " + want
                + " — an editor comparing versions will not offer this to anyone holding an older "
                + declared });

// The version a tool actually reads is the one in the manifest, built by the packer from `package.json`.
// Two files, so they can disagree.
let mutable before: [Int] = [];
match file_read_bytes(VSIX) {
    None => {
        print_error("cannot read " + VSIX + " — run this from the repository root");
        exit(2);
    }
    Some(bytes) => { before = bytes; }
}
let mutable entries: [ZipRead] = [];
let count: Int = zip_entries(before, entries);
if count < 0 {
    print_error(VSIX + " is not a readable archive");
    exit(1);
}
let mutable manifest: String = "";
let mutable packed_json: String = "";
let mutable e: Int = 0;
while e < len(entries) {
    if entries[e].name == "extension.vsixmanifest" || entries[e].name == "extension/package.json" {
        let mutable bytes: [Int] = [];
        let got: Int = zip_content(before, entries[e], bytes);
        if got < 0 {
            print_error(VSIX + ": " + entries[e].name + " will not come out (" + to_string(got) + ")");
            exit(1);
        }
        if entries[e].name == "extension.vsixmanifest" {
            manifest = from_bytes(bytes);
        } else {
            packed_json = from_bytes(bytes);
        }
    }
    e += 1;
}
let packed: String = json_text_field(packed_json, "version");
let identity: String = identity_version(manifest);
let mutable shown_identity: String = identity;
if len(identity) == 0 {
    shown_identity = "(none)";
}
let k2: Int = push(claims, Claim {
        ok: len(identity) > 0 && identity == packed && packed == source_version,
        ok_line: "the manifest, the packaged manifest and the source agree on " + packed,
        fail_line: "the manifest says " + shown_identity + ", the packaged package.json says " + packed
                + ", and the source says " + source_version });

// **Reproducibility first, because it is what makes the next check mean anything.**
//
// Asserted as a property of the archive rather than by packing twice and comparing. The version of this
// that packed twice in a row PASSED on a packer that stamped the current time in three entries — a zip
// stores timestamps at two-second granularity, so back-to-back runs land in the same bucket. It took a
// three-second sleep to expose it. **A test that can only fail when it happens to straddle a boundary is a
// test that reports success.** One fixed stamp for every entry cannot pass by accident.
let mutable stamps: [String] = [];
let mutable s: Int = 0;
while s < len(entries) {
    if !has(stamps, stamp_text(entries[s])) {
        let p: Int = push(stamps, stamp_text(entries[s]));
    }
    s += 1;
}
if prove {
    if !has(stamps, "(2026, 8, 18, 19, 48, 36)") {
        let p: Int = push(stamps, "(2026, 8, 18, 19, 48, 36)");
    }
}
let sorted_stamps: [String] = sorted_unique(stamps);
let mutable first_stamp: String = "";
if len(sorted_stamps) > 0 {
    first_stamp = sorted_stamps[0];
}
let k3: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(sorted_stamps) == 1,
        ok_line: "every entry carries one fixed timestamp " + first_stamp
                + ", so two packs give one artefact",
        fail_line: "entries carry " + to_string(len(sorted_stamps))
                + " different timestamps, so the bytes move on every run and a stale commit cannot be"
                + " detected: [" + string_join(sorted_stamps, ", ") + "]" });

// **And the committed artefact is the one the packer writes.** Compared against a repack, which is what
// nothing did before: CI packs and then inspects the result, so it overwrites the evidence one line before
// looking for it. **A check that regenerates the artefact it verifies cannot see a stale one.** The packed
// bytes are restored afterwards either way, so this never leaves a dirty tree.
let mutable packer: String = os_env_or("BMX_PACK", "");
if len(packer) == 0 {
    match file_temp_directory("bmx-extension") {
        None => {
            print_error("cannot make a scratch directory");
            exit(2);
        }
        Some(scratch) => {
            let built: String = scratch + "/pack";
            let made: Capture = os_capture_status("burxt build " + file_quote(PACK) + " -o "
                    + file_quote(built));
            if made.code != 0 {
                print_error("cannot build the packer this check drives:");
                print_error(made.output + made.errors);
                exit(1);
            }
            packer = built;
        }
    }
}
let ran: Capture = os_capture_status(packer);
if ran.code != 0 {
    print_error("the packer refused:");
    print_error(ran.output + ran.errors);
    exit(1);
}
let mutable after: [Int] = [];
match file_read_bytes(VSIX) {
    None => {
        print_error("the packer wrote no archive");
        exit(1);
    }
    Some(bytes) => { after = bytes; }
}
let restored: Int = write_bytes(VSIX, before);
let mutable same: Bool = len(before) == len(after);
if same {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(before) && same {
        if before[i] != after[i] {
            same = false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
}
if prove {
    same = false;
}
let k4: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: same,
        ok_line: "the committed .vsix is what the packer writes (" + to_string(len(before)) + " bytes)",
        fail_line: "the committed .vsix is not what the packer writes — it was changed without"
                + " repacking" });

// **And the archive is a well-formed extension, asserted HERE rather than by a second step that packs.**
// `tools/check.sh` used to run the packer again to inspect the result, three lines after the comparison
// above — so a stale artefact was detected and then silently repaired inside one invocation, and the
// second local run was always green. Guarding that with a park-and-restore worked and was the wrong fix:
// **the collision is eliminated by having exactly one thing pack.**
//
// Read from the bytes the packer just wrote rather than from the file, so nothing on disk is touched at
// all. The entry list is written out rather than globbed, because a missing entry is only ever found by
// packaging.
let mutable need: [String] = [];
let n1: Int = push(need, "[Content_Types].xml");
let n2: Int = push(need, "extension.vsixmanifest");
let n3: Int = push(need, "extension/package.json");
let n4: Int = push(need, "extension/syntaxes/bmx.tmLanguage.json");
let n5: Int = push(need, "extension/icon.png");
if prove {
    let n6: Int = push(need, "extension/a-file-the-packer-never-writes");
}
let mutable fresh: [ZipRead] = [];
let fresh_count: Int = zip_entries(after, fresh);
let mutable present: [String] = [];
let mutable p: Int = 0;
while p < len(fresh) {
    let x: Int = push(present, fresh[p].name);
    p += 1;
}
let mutable missing_entries: [String] = [];
let mutable q: Int = 0;
while q < len(need) {
    if !has(present, need[q]) {
        let x: Int = push(missing_entries, need[q]);
    }
    q += 1;
}
let k5: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(missing_entries) == 0,
        ok_line: "the packed archive carries all " + to_string(len(need))
                + " entries an editor needs, and is not corrupt",
        fail_line: "the packed archive is missing ['" + string_join(missing_entries, "', '") + "']" });

// Every entry, out through its LOCAL header and against its own CRC — which is the copy of the metadata
// `zipfile.testzip()` never reads.
let mutable unreadable: Int = 0;
if fresh_count < 0 {
    unreadable = 1;
} else {
    let mutable r: Int = 0;
    while r < len(fresh) {
        let mutable bytes: [Int] = [];
        if zip_content(after, fresh[r], bytes) < 0 {
            unreadable += 1;
        }
        r += 1;
    }
}
let k6: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: unreadable == 0,
        ok_line: "the archive is readable end to end", fail_line: "the archive is corrupt" });

// **The language server had the same defect, found by grepping for the version I had just removed.** It
// answered `initialize` with `serverInfo: {name: 'bmx-lsp', version: '0.1.0'}` while reporting 0.12
// diagnostics, and `editors/lsp/test/protocol.mjs` checked the NAME — the half that never changes — and
// not the version. A client puts `serverInfo` in its log, so a stale one misdirects the first question
// anybody asks about a diagnostic: which version produced it.
// **The file and the pattern both moved when the server was ported to Burxt**, and this check is how I
// found out: it read `bmx-lsp.mjs`, which no longer exists, and the suite failed on a path rather than on
// a version. A check keyed to a filename is a check that breaks when the file is right to rename — which
// is the cheap kind of breakage, because it says so immediately.
let lsp: String = must_read("editors/lsp/bmx-lsp.bx");
let mutable served: String = "";
// `json_field\("version",\s*json_text\("([^"]+)"\)\)` — the whitespace matters, because a long line in
// this file may well be broken after the comma one day, and **an offset counted by hand does not.** The
// first cut of this used `mark + 32` where the needle is 33 bytes, so it read the closing quote as the
// version, found an empty string, and reported the server as having no version at all — a check that
// fails LOUDLY on an arithmetic slip, which is the good kind, and the reason the port compared outputs.
let opener: String = "json_field(\"version\",";
let mark: Int = string_find(lsp, opener);
if mark >= 0 {
    let mutable i: Int = mark + len(opener);
    while i < len(lsp) && string_is_space(byte_at(lsp, i)) {
        i += 1;
    }
    let inner: String = "json_text(\"";
    if string_matches_at(lsp, inner, i) {
        let start: Int = i + len(inner);
        let ends: Int = string_find_from(lsp, "\"", start);
        if ends > start {
            served = substring(lsp, start, ends - start);
        }
    }
}
if prove {
    served = "0.1.0";
}
let mutable shown_served: String = served;
if len(served) == 0 {
    shown_served = "None";
}
let k7: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(served) > 0 && string_starts_with(served, want + "."),
        ok_line: "the language server answers `initialize` with " + shown_served,
        fail_line: "the language server reports version " + shown_served + " while producing " + want
                + " diagnostics — a client logs that, so every bug report about it names the wrong"
                + " version" });

// And the name every document hands to a reader.
//
// **Only names on an `install-extension` line, because the promise IS the install command.** This scanned
// every `.vsix` token on every page, which cannot tell **using** a name from **mentioning** one — so a
// sentence recording the old scheme (*"the artefact used to be called `bmx-0.1.0.vsix`"*) failed the check
// for describing history accurately. Proven reachable by appending exactly that sentence to
// `editors/vscode/README.md` and watching this fire; latent only because no page happens to carry it
// today. A check should not constrain what the documentation may say about its own past.
//
// Internal paths — the archive-reading lines in `check.sh` and `ci.yml` — are deliberately not scanned.
// They are not promises to a reader, and they are self-checking: opening a missing zip throws.
let all_pages: [String] = pages();
let mutable named_raw: [String] = [];
let mutable g: Int = 0;
while g < len(all_pages) {
    let body: String = must_read(all_pages[g]);
    let found: [String] = promised(body, ours);
    let mutable h: Int = 0;
    while h < len(found) {
        let x: Int = push(named_raw, found[h]);
        h += 1;
    }
    g += 1;
}
if prove {
    // Through `promised`, not straight into the list: a control that skips the extractor proves the
    // comparison works and says nothing about whether the extractor still sees an install line.
    let found: [String] = promised("code --install-extension bmx-0.1.0.vsix", ours);
    let mutable h: Int = 0;
    while h < len(found) {
        let x: Int = push(named_raw, found[h]);
        h += 1;
    }
}
let named: [String] = sorted_unique(named_raw);
let mutable missing_named: [String] = [];
let mutable m: Int = 0;
while m < len(named) {
    if !file_is_file("editors/vscode/" + named[m]) {
        let x: Int = push(missing_named, named[m]);
    }
    m += 1;
}
let k8: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(missing_named) == 0,
        ok_line: "every .vsix the documentation names exists (" + string_join(named, ", ") + ")",
        fail_line: "the documentation tells someone to install " + string_join(missing_named, ", ")
                + ", which is not in the tree" });

// The path half of the same promise. A directory is not a file, so the check above cannot see it.
let mutable bare: [String] = [];
let mutable teaches: [String] = [];
let mutable t: Int = 0;
while t < len(all_pages) {
    let mutable body: String = must_read(all_pages[t]);
    if prove && all_pages[t] == "editors/README.md" {
        // The control is the defect as it was written: the local directory, alone, in an instruction.
        body = string_replace(body, REMOTE, "~/.vscode/extensions-CONTROL");
    }
    if string_contains(body, "install-extension") {
        let x: Int = push(teaches, all_pages[t]);
    }
    // A page may mention the local directory freely — it must not be the ONLY one it names, because a
    // reader in a remote window follows it into a directory nothing reads.
    if string_contains(body, LOCAL) && !string_contains(body, REMOTE) {
        let y: Int = push(bare, all_pages[t]);
    }
    t += 1;
}
let k9: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(bare) == 0,
        ok_line: "no page sends a reader to " + LOCAL + " without naming " + REMOTE + " beside it",
        fail_line: string_join(bare, ", ") + " names " + LOCAL
                + " alone, which is a directory nothing reads in a remote window" });
let k10: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(teaches) > 0,
        ok_line: "the install instruction every page leads with is `code --install-extension` ("
                + to_string(len(teaches)) + " " + plural(len(teaches), "page") + ")",
        fail_line: "no page names `code --install-extension`, so every reader is left resolving the"
                + " extensions directory themselves" });

// **The packer's own usage line, which promised a filename it stopped writing.** It said
// `writes bmx-<version>.vsix here` while the code twelve lines from the end wrote `bmx.vsix` — stale in the
// one file whose long comment explains why the version was taken OUT of the filename.
//
// **Two checks missed it for the same reason:** `bmx-<version>.vsix` contains `<` and `>`, which are not in
// `[\w.-]`, so the name-scan above never matched the placeholder at all — not before the install-extension
// scoping and not after.
//
// So the promise is compared to the behaviour rather than to a pattern — the usage line's filename against
// the name the packer actually builds. No wording heuristic, and a placeholder cannot slip through,
// because a placeholder is not equal to a real name.
let builds: String = ours + ".vsix";
let mutable promised_out: String = usage_promise(must_read(PACK));
if prove {
    promised_out = "bmx-<version>.vsix";
}
let k11: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: promised_out == builds,
        ok_line: "the packer's usage line promises " + builds + ", which is what it writes",
        fail_line: "the packer's usage line promises " + promised_out + " but it writes " + builds });

// A leftover from the old scheme is a second answer to "which one do I install".
let mutable stale: [String] = [];
match file_list_directory("editors/vscode") {
    None => { }
    Some(found) => {
        let mutable i: Int = 0;
        while i < len(found) {
            if string_starts_with(found[i], "bmx-") && string_ends_with(found[i], ".vsix") {
                let x: Int = push(stale, found[i]);
            }
            i += 1;
        }
    }
}
let stale_sorted: [String] = sorted_unique(stale);
let k12: Int = push(claims, Claim { ok: len(stale_sorted) == 0,
        ok_line: "no versioned .vsix is left over from the old naming",
        fail_line: string_join(stale_sorted, ", ") + " is still in the tree beside bmx.vsix" });

let mutable failures: Int = 0;
let mutable c: Int = 0;
while c < len(claims) {
    if claims[c].ok {
        print("  ok    " + claims[c].ok_line);
    } else {
        failures += 1;
        print("  FAIL  " + claims[c].fail_line);
    }
    c += 1;
}

print("");
if prove {
    if failures > 0 {
        print("the control failed as it must — an unbumped version, a stale artefact, a documented"
                + " filename that does not exist, an install path nothing reads and a missing archive"
                + " entry are all caught");
        exit(0);
    }
    print("THE CONTROL DID NOT FAIL, so this check cannot see the defect it exists for");
    exit(1);
}
if failures > 0 {
    print(to_string(failures) + " " + plural(failures, "thing") + " wrong with the packaged extension");
    exit(1);
}
print("every editor artefact declares " + declared + ", packed from source, named the same thing"
        + " everywhere");
exit(0);
